+## 27 November 2026 — established plugin systems and engineering lessons
+
+**Evidence boundary.** The original AQE/Nagual/Quality Forge research is retained. The comparisons below add independently designed ecosystems using primary producer documentation and official incident/advisory reports retrieved on 27 November 2026. A documented mechanism is evidence that an approach exists, not proof that it produced superior engineering or human outcomes. Incident reports substantiate a reported failure and response; they do not establish how often it occurs or that Tesbo has the same vulnerability. No comparative benchmark or independently measured benefit in Tesbo was performed. The adoption column is a scoped inference for the proposed design, not a dependency choice.
+
+| Primary evidence | Documented mechanism, outcome or pitfall | Distilled principle and Tesbo disposition | Limit / counterexample |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| [Backstage extension points](https://backstage.io/docs/backend-system/architecture/extension-points/) and [modules](https://backstage.io/docs/backend-system/architecture/modules/) | Small public extension interfaces; duplicate action IDs and competing singleton runners are rejected. Module initialization precedes plugin initialization and constrains later registration. | **Adapt:** define namespaced contributions, allowed multiplicity, collision refusal and actual activation/version boundaries at existing native owners. Test duplicate/reserved IDs and an allowed multi-provider point. | Do not import Backstage's architecture, database layout or initialization implementation. These docs do not establish dynamic hot reload or measured reliability in Tesbo. |
+| [VS Code contribution points](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/references/contribution-points) and [extension host](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/advanced-topics/extension-host) | Distinct contribution declarations and runtime/location requirements; activation can be lazy and extensions do not directly own the editor's UI. | **Adapt:** compare declared with actually registered/invoked capabilities; load only needed capabilities within the selected profile and measured operating envelope. | A process or declaration is not a permission boundary. Do not select a desktop runtime or require every profile to be executable code. Performance benefit here is a design rationale, not a Tesbo measurement. |
+| [VS Code runtime security](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/configure/extensions/extension-runtime-security) | The extension host has VS Code's permissions, including files, network and process execution; publisher/dependency trust requires explicit consideration. | **Adapt the distinction, reject the ambient authority:** package trust/admission does not grant tenant/project/target rights. Use actual authority and isolation controls for admitted effects. | Desktop-user trust does not transfer to hostile multi-tenant server execution. No manifest-only sandbox claim. |
+| [Microsoft's extension supply-chain response](https://github.com/microsoft/vsmarketplace/discussions/1904) | The producer reports removing an affected extension version within hours and adding delayed automatic updates/cooldown in VS Code 1.123. | **Evaluate:** bind containment and update decisions to exact package/dependency versions, not publisher reputation alone; preserve attribution and affected-scope recovery. | This is an attributable producer incident account, not an independently reproduced outcome or an order to copy automatic updates/cooldown. Tesbo update/distribution policy remains unselected. |
+| [Jenkins plugin management](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/managing/plugins/) | Removing a required dependency can prevent correct startup. Disable, uninstall and purging old configuration are different operations; uninstall can retain configuration. | **Adapt:** inspect dependency impact before transition and preserve an explicit removal/data policy. Test required/optional dependencies, unaffected consumers and usable historical results. | Jenkins restart/configuration semantics are not Tesbo requirements; retained data alone does not ensure a usable reader or complete recovery. |
+| [Grafana security release](https://grafana.com/blog/grafana-security-releases-new-versions-with-fixes-for-cve-2022-39229-cve-2022-39201-cve-2022-31130-cve-2022-31123/) | Official disclosures identify proxy cookie/token leaks and a plugin-signature bypass through a versioning flaw. | **Adapt:** explicitly constrain credential routing and artifact/version admission; assert actual outbound bytes and negative integrity/compatibility controls. | A signature is not authorization or proof of safe behavior. These historical flaws are not findings in Tesbo or claims that current Grafana is affected. |
+| [Backstage connection concepts](https://backstage.io/docs/backend-system/core-services/connections/concepts/) | Plugin-scoped configuration narrows visible credentials; the docs explicitly say this does not replace authorization. | **Adapt:** keep connection selection/configuration separate from current caller/target authority, including shared-account and departing-owner cases. | Do not infer that installing an extension or having a matching account configuration permits an action. |
+| [Terraform plugin protocol](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/plugin/terraform-plugin-protocol) and [go-plugin](https://github.com/hashicorp/go-plugin) | Explicit protocol compatibility; go-plugin uses RPC with a reliable local-network assumption. | **Adapt:** version the actual host/contribution contract and reject incompatible negotiation. Compare an existing adapter before a new SDK. | Neither gRPC nor Go nor a local-process reliability model is selected. A remote Internet service needs its actual failure, identity and network contracts. |
+| [VS Code Webviews](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/extension-guides/webview) and [UX guidance](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/ux-guidelines/webviews) | Custom views have specific resource, message, sanitization/CSP, theming and accessibility obligations; custom UI is not always necessary. | **Adapt:** prefer adequate native presentation; qualify admitted custom UI boundaries and retained historical meaning after its renderer disappears. | Product-specific browser restrictions are not a complete Tesbo threat model; documented accessibility guidance is not observed human usability. |
+| [Forge app versions](https://developer.atlassian.com/platform/forge/versions/) and [storage lifecycle](https://developer.atlassian.com/platform/forge/storage-reference/hosted-storage-data-lifecycle/) | Installation/version and consent changes are distinct; retained uninstall data is not a generally readable archive or automatic recovery. | **Adapt:** evaluate material authority changes separately from package version labels; define lawful readable history, export/migration and recovery before admitting persistent custom formats. | Forge's retention periods, approval model and support process do not become Tesbo policy. No recovery success rate was measured here. |
+| [Current Grafana Frontend Sandbox status](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/administration/plugin-management/plugin-frontend-sandbox/) | The public-preview feature is documented as deprecated and scheduled for removal in 13.4. | **Reject as a presently selected mechanism:** check lifecycle/maintenance before adopting an attractive precedent; retain only the relevant problem/constraint as research. | This does not reject all browser isolation designs or decide Tesbo's runtime. A dated source needs rechecking when adoption is considered. |
+
+### Decisions changed by this comparison
+
+The proposed v1.4.49 contract now distinguishes package dependencies, work-unit dependencies and application assurance relationships; it adds namespaced contribution conflicts, explicit provider selection/default binding, compatible state recovery, usable historical representation and profile-specific effects. It applies existing revocation, stopping, budgets and native evidence controls rather than reproducing them in a second plugin framework. Installation, connection, project enablement and actual grant remain separate, including the actor who can resolve each blocked state. The research has not selected a first profile, marketplace, SDK, executable/UI runtime or automatic update policy.
+
+**Stopping point for this design increment.** These sources support concrete proposed boundaries and falsifiable acceptance dimensions. Additional broad searching is unlikely to resolve the remaining product choices without a selected user outcome. Targeted deeper source/incident/version/license analysis resumes when a profile or dependency is considered for implementation, or when a relied-on assumption changes. Runtime controls and historical-readability cases remain NOT RUN. Existing functionality, configuration, a native adapter or no change remain valid alternatives to installing a plugin.
+
+**Reusable engineering lesson.** Investigate established outcomes and failures, separate the evidence from the inferred mechanism, identify applicability/counterexamples, and test the contextual decision through the existing owner. The detailed portable method belongs to canonical 06 and the existing engineering/team instructions. This research document supplies a worked application, not another binding team charter or an installed Skill.
